      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The question rebuilds Nino 3.4 anomalies on two different climatology baselines and applies the ONI persistence criterion, so this fragment governs anomaly definition, baseline dependence, Nino 3.4/ONI, and event counting.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Build each series against the climatology of the field's **own** source and the baseline of the published series it is compared with (`base_period`; `compute_anomaly_vs_own_climatology` / `compute_enso_nino_index`): the ERSSTv5 anomaly must come from ERSSTv5's own monthly climatology, since forcing an observed climatology onto a field puts that field's mean-state bias into the index; a monthly index is matched to the climatology of its own calendar month, which removes the seasonal cycle; state the climatology and baseline used (the tools record both in the result's attrs).",
        "Anomalies on different baselines differ by one constant per calendar month even when variability is identical, so the offset between RECON_8110 and RECON_9120 (and between the two CPC series) is an offset, not an error of the reconstruction; count events operationally by persistence with `min_duration` = 5 consecutive 3-month seasons beyond the threshold (`detect_threshold_events`) — a single step beyond threshold is noise."
      ]
    },
